            When You Need One and How to Choose
          </h1>
          <p class="byline">By Aleksi Suon, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant · Updated November 2026</p>
          <p class="hero__sub">
            You can file and appear in a Dubai divorce case yourself, and a close relative can appear for you.
            A lawyer earns the fee when children, money or a disputed claim are involved, when your spouse has
            already filed, or when you live outside the UAE. Below: who may represent you in Dubai, how to check a
            lawyer, and what the court and the lawyer cost. DivorceU.AE is a referral service, not a law firm: we
            put you in touch with a licensed lawyer, and you decide whether to instruct them.
          </p>
          <ul class="hero__trust">
            <li>✓ Referral service, not a law firm</li>
            <li>✓ UAE-licensed family lawyers</li>
            <li>✓ English &amp; Arabic</li>
            <li>✓ Free first consultation</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="hero__form">
          <LeadForm lang="en" variant="hero" heading="Speak to a Divorce Lawyer in Dubai" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="container">

      <!-- ===== DO YOU NEED A LAWYER ===== -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="need-heading">
        <h2 id="need-heading">Do You Need a Divorce Lawyer in Dubai?</h2>
        <p>
          Not by law, but often in practice. Article 52(1) of the Civil Procedure Code, Federal Decree-Law
          No. 42 of 2022, lets you appear in person or through an attorney: a lawyer, or a relative or in-law
          up to the fourth degree. Article 8(1) of Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022 on the legal profession
          gives the same option. In a divorce your spouse is the other side, so in practice this means another
          relative or in-law.
        </p>

        <h3>When you can reasonably manage without a lawyer</h3>
        <ul class="list">
          <li>You both agree on the divorce and on everything that follows from it.</li>
          <li>There are no children, or their arrangements are agreed and simple.</li>
          <li>There is no property, business or significant savings to divide or claim.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Even then, consider paying for a single review before you sign. For Muslim couples, a settlement
          reached in Family Guidance and approved by the supervising judge has the force of an execution writ
          and cannot be appealed unless it contradicts the law (Article 8(2) of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of
          2024).
        </p>

        <h3>When a lawyer is worth paying for</h3>
        <ul class="list">
          <li>Custody, visitation, a child’s travel, alimony, housing or a deferred dowry is disputed.</li>
          <li>Your spouse has already filed: the defence is due within 10 business days of service (Article 48(2) of the Civil Procedure Code).</li>
          <li>You live outside the UAE: a plaintiff with no domicile in the State must name an elected domicile and a legal representative (Article 44(2)(a)).</li>
          <li>Your spouse has left the country or cannot be found.</li>
          <li>You face violence or threats at home and need urgent orders.</li>
          <li>You will need the divorce recognised in another country.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- ===== WHO MAY REPRESENT YOU ===== -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="who-heading">
        <h2 id="who-heading">Who May Represent You in a Dubai Divorce Case</h2>
        <p>
          Divorce lawyers in Dubai work within Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022, which regulates the legal
          profession and the separate legal consultation profession. Under Article 6, only lawyers licensed in
          the State may give legal opinion and advice, use the title "lawyer" and appear for clients before the
          courts, the prosecution and the police; courts admit representation only by lawyers on the Roll of
          Practising Lawyers. Article 3(2) lets emirates with their own judiciary, such as Dubai, regulate the
          profession locally, and the Government of Dubai Legal Affairs Department keeps separate rolls for
          advocates and for legal consultants (Administrative Resolution No. 51 of 2022).
        </p>
        <p>
          Two further federal rules shape who you will meet. A practising lawyer must be a UAE national
          (Article 13(1)), and the narrow route for licensing non-national lawyers (15 years or more of
          practice, as a partner of a licensed foreign firm, specialised circuits only) excludes family and
          Muslim personal status proceedings (Article 10). Legal consultants work only through a law firm and
          cannot plead, represent anyone in court, sign pleadings or sign fee agreements (Articles 59 and 61).
        </p>

        <div class="cost-table-wrapper">
          <table class="cost-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Who</th>
                <th>Can give you legal advice</th>
                <th>Can appear for you in a Dubai family case</th>
                <th>Where registered</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Advocate (practising lawyer)</strong></td>
                <td>Yes (Art. 6)</td>
                <td>Yes</td>
                <td>Advocates’ roll, Government of Dubai Legal Affairs Department</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Legal consultant</strong></td>
                <td>Provides legal services, but only through a law firm (Art. 59)</td>
                <td>No: cannot plead, sign pleadings or sign a fee agreement (Arts. 59 and 61)</td>
                <td>Legal consultants’ roll, Government of Dubai Legal Affairs Department</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Non-national lawyer licensed under Art. 10</strong></td>
                <td>Within the licence</td>
                <td>No: family and Muslim personal status proceedings are excluded (Art. 10)</td>
                <td>Licensed through the federal law’s Article 10 route</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Relative or in-law up to the fourth degree</strong></td>
                <td>No, they are not a licensed adviser</td>
                <td>Yes, if you authorise them (Art. 8(1); Civil Procedure Code Art. 52)</td>
                <td>Not registered; acts on your authorisation</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>You</strong></td>
                <td>Not applicable</td>
                <td>Yes, in person (Civil Procedure Code Art. 52(1))</td>
                <td>Not applicable</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">Articles refer to Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022 unless the Civil Procedure Code is named.</p>

        <p>
          The English-speaking family lawyer you meet first at a Dubai firm may be registered as a legal
          consultant. That is fine for work done within the firm, but the person who signs your pleadings and appears
          at the Personal Status Court must be an advocate on the roll. Ask at the first meeting: who will
          appear at my hearings, and what is their registration number?
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ===== HOW TO CHECK A LAWYER ===== -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="check-heading">
        <h2 id="check-heading">How to Check a Dubai Divorce Lawyer Before You Sign</h2>
        <p>
          Six checks, each tied to a rule in Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022, are worth doing before you sign
          anything or pay a deposit.
        </p>

        <h3>1. Name, registration number and roll</h3>
        <p>
          Ask for the lawyer’s full name as registered, the registration number and which roll it is on.
          Advocates and legal consultants sit on different rolls kept by the Legal Affairs Department, so
          "registered" alone does not tell you whether the person can appear in court. If the details do not
          match, contact the Legal Affairs Department before you pay anything.
        </p>

        <h3>2. A written fee agreement before any work</h3>
        <p>
          Article 46(2) requires a written fee agreement before the lawyer starts work. Check which stages it
          covers (first instance, appeal, cassation, enforcement), whether court fees, translation and
          attestation are included, and how and when you pay. A legal consultant cannot sign it (Article 61).
        </p>

        <h3>3. How the fee is set</h3>
        <p>
          Article 47 lists the factors against which a lawyer’s fee is assessed. Ask the lawyer to tie the
          quote to your case: which claims it covers, how many hearings they expect, and what would trigger an
          extra charge.
        </p>

        <h3>4. What happens if you change lawyer</h3>
        <p>
          Article 57 deals with the fees owed when a client dismisses a lawyer before the work is finished. Ask
          how your agreement applies it before you sign, so a later change of lawyer does not bring a surprise
          bill.
        </p>

        <h3>5. One lawyer cannot act for both spouses</h3>
        <p>
          Article 45(1)(f) bars a lawyer from helping the client’s opponent in the same dispute, even with
          advice. An offer to "handle the agreed divorce for both of you" is an offer the law prohibits. In an
          agreed divorce, one spouse can instruct a lawyer to draft the settlement and the other can pay a
          separate lawyer to review it.
        </p>

        <h3>6. Confidentiality</h3>
        <p>
          Article 45(1)(a) obliges a lawyer to keep a client’s information confidential, and Article 44 bars a
          lawyer from testifying about information learned from a client. Tell your lawyer the difficult facts
          early, such as debts, messages or assets abroad.
        </p>

        <h3>Questions to ask at the first consultation</h3>
        <ol class="ordered-list">
          <li>Which law applies to us, and which court is competent?</li>
          <li>Who will appear at my hearings, and are they on the advocates’ roll?</li>
          <li>Do I need anything urgent now, such as temporary alimony or an order about the children’s travel?</li>
          <li>What does your fee cover, stage by stage, and what costs extra?</li>
          <li>What outcomes are realistic for a case like mine, and what are the risks?</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Red flags</h3>
        <ul class="list">
          <li>A guaranteed result or a promised finish date.</li>
          <li>Full payment demanded with no written agreement.</li>
          <li>Refusal to give a registration number or to say who will appear.</li>
          <li>Pressure to sign a settlement before you understand it.</li>
          <li>An offer to act for both spouses.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For a longer checklist on comparing lawyers across emirates, read
          <a href="/en/how-to-choose-a-divorce-lawyer-uae/">how to choose a divorce lawyer in the UAE</a>.
        </p>

        <div class="cta-inline">
          <p>Want a licensed Dubai family lawyer to look at your case?</p>
          <a href="#contact" class="btn btn--primary">Request a Free Consultation</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ===== WHICH LAW APPLIES ===== -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="law-heading">
        <h2 id="law-heading">Which Law Applies: Muslim, Non-Muslim and Mixed Couples in Dubai</h2>
        <p>
          The first thing a Dubai divorce lawyer settles is which law governs your case, because that decides
          the route, the grounds, what you can claim and how custody works. For family cases, the Dubai Courts
          apply two federal laws. The DIFC Courts have no family jurisdiction and cannot grant a divorce.
        </p>

        <h3>Muslim couples: Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024</h3>
        <p>
          The Personal Status Law, in force since 15 April 2025, applies to UAE citizens where both spouses or
          one of them is Muslim, and to non-citizens unless one of them insists on applying his or her own law
          or another permitted law (Article 1). It also applies to divorce proceedings with no final judgment
          when it came into force.
        </p>
        <p>
          Under Article 8, the supervising judge may refer the spouses to Family Guidance before a case goes to
          court if he or she considers a settlement feasible. It is a discretion, not a fixed stage, and urgent
          and temporary matters (including temporary orders on alimony and custody) and cases to prove a divorce
          are exempt. In practice, Dubai Courts’ service for filing a personal status claim asks for the Family
          Conciliation referral to be uploaded with the claim, and the conciliation service has no fee. Where
          the court appoints arbitrators in a contested case of discord, they have at most 60 days from their
          appointment (Article 72).
        </p>

        <h3>Non-Muslim couples: Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022</h3>
        <p>
          The civil personal status law applies to non-Muslim UAE nationals and non-Muslim foreigners residing
          in the State, unless one spouse adheres to the law of their home country (Article 1). Either spouse
          may ask for divorce on the prescribed form without proving harm or blame (Articles 7 and 8), the
          divorce takes effect by court decision after the other spouse is served, and the case is not referred
          to Family Guidance (Article 3).
        </p>
        <p>
          Custody after divorce is a joint and equal right of both parents unless the court is asked to give it
          to one of them or a parent waives it in writing (Article 10). Alimony is claimed after the divorce
          judgment and decided at the judge’s discretion against listed factors such as the years of marriage
          and the wife’s age (Article 9). There is no automatic equal split of assets. More detail is in
          <a href="/en/non-muslim-divorce-uae/">non-Muslim divorce in the UAE</a>.
        </p>

        <h3>Mixed couples</h3>
        <p>
          The 2022 law is written for non-Muslims. When one spouse is Muslim, the starting point is the 2024
          law, which covers UAE citizens where one spouse is Muslim and non-citizens generally; for
          non-citizens, Article 1(3) still lets a spouse insist on his or her own national law. Settle which
          law applies with the lawyer at the first meeting rather than assume it.
        </p>

        <h3>Should a Dubai resident file in Abu Dhabi instead?</h3>
        <p>
          Some non-Muslim couples in Dubai ask about the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court, which applies Abu
          Dhabi’s own civil family legislation, runs its proceedings in English and charges AED 5,000 for a
          civil divorce application. Whether it can hear your case depends on its jurisdiction rules and your
          connection to Abu Dhabi, so have a lawyer confirm that before you pay, and do not choose it on the
          assumption that it will be faster. For a case under the 2024 law, Article 3 ties the competent court
          to where the respondent lives or works, and for divorce, alimony and custody cases brought by a wife,
          also to where she lives or works or the marital home. If you both live in Dubai, that points to Dubai.
          The Abu Dhabi side is explained in <a href="/en/family-court-abu-dhabi/">family court Abu Dhabi</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ===== DUBAI COURT SIDE ===== -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="court-heading">
        <h2 id="court-heading">The Dubai Court Side: What Your Lawyer Handles</h2>
        <p>
          The Personal Status Court sits with the Inheritance Court in Deira, Al Garhoud area, Street 5, next to
          the RTA main building (Makani 3427491952). Dubai Courts lists its hours as Monday to Thursday 8:00 am
          to 3:00 pm and Friday 8:00 am to 11:30 am; the courts are closed at weekends and on public holidays,
          so check before you go. The call centre is +971 4 334 7777, and claims can be filed online or through
          Al Adheed service centres (600 523 433). The building, services and first visit are covered
          in the <a href="/en/family-court-dubai/">Dubai family court guide</a>.
        </p>

        <h3>What happens, step by step</h3>
        <ol class="ordered-list">
          <li><strong>Choosing the route.</strong> The governing law, the competent court, agreed or contested, and whether an urgent application comes first.</li>
          <li><strong>Documents.</strong> Dubai Courts asks for PDFs with a legal Arabic translation. Foreign documents are legalised by the issuing country’s foreign ministry, then the UAE embassy there, then the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs.</li>
          <li><strong>Family Conciliation or direct filing.</strong> For a Muslim couple, the claim service asks for the Family Conciliation referral; your lawyer will know whether your matter is one Article 8 exempts. A non-Muslim civil divorce goes straight to the court.</li>
          <li><strong>Filing and fees.</strong> The claim is filed electronically or on paper (Article 44(1) of the Civil Procedure Code), through the Dubai Courts website or an Al Adheed centre; individuals are reported to sign in with UAE PASS, so confirm on the site. The fee must be paid within 3 business days, counted from the day after the payment notice, or the registration is void (Article 47(1)).</li>
          <li><strong>Service and defence.</strong> The other spouse can be served by recorded call, SMS, smart applications, email or other modern means (Article 9(1)(a) of the Civil Procedure Code) and has 10 business days from service to file a defence (Article 48(2)).</li>
          <li><strong>Hearings.</strong> Steps done wholly or partly remotely are valid (Article 330), and any party may ask at each remote hearing for the trial to be held in person (Article 333).</li>
          <li><strong>Judgment and enforcement.</strong> The first-instance judgment is issued electronically. If the other side does not comply, the lawyer opens an enforcement file.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Dubai Courts fees for a family case</h3>
        <p>
          Court fees are set by Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015 concerning the judicial fees of the Dubai Courts. For
          personal status matters they are low, so the lawyer, not the court, is most of the bill.
        </p>
        <div class="cost-table-wrapper">
          <table class="cost-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Item</th>
                <th>Official fee</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Personal status application (first instance)</td><td>AED 100 per application, capped at AED 2,000</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Appeal</td><td>AED 100</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Alimony appeal</td><td>Free</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Cassation</td><td>AED 2,000</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Travel ban or order on petition</td><td>AED 100</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Enforcement file</td><td>AED 50</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Family Conciliation service</td><td>No fee</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">Source: Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015 and Dubai Courts service pages. Confirm the current fee at registration. Translation, attestation and lawyer fees are separate.</p>

        <h3>Appeals</h3>
        <p>
          For cases under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, the period to appeal or to file in cassation is 30
          days (Article 9). It runs from the day after the judgment if it was issued in your presence,
          otherwise from the day after you are notified (Article 10). For a civil judgment under the 2022 law,
          ask your lawyer for the exact deadline on the day the judgment is issued.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ===== COST ===== -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="cost-heading">
        <h2 id="cost-heading">How Much Does a Divorce Lawyer Cost in Dubai?</h2>
        <p>
          It depends on the lawyer and the case, and we do not publish a price list we cannot source. The fee is
          agreed between you and the lawyer in writing before work starts (Article 46(2) of Federal Decree-Law
          No. 34 of 2022), and Article 47 lists the factors a fee is assessed against.
        </p>

        <h3>What drives the fee</h3>
        <ul class="list">
          <li><strong>Agreed or contested.</strong> Drafting a settlement is a smaller job than a contested case with evidence and several hearings.</li>
          <li><strong>The number of claims.</strong> Custody, visitation, travel, alimony, child maintenance, housing and deferred dowry can each need its own application.</li>
          <li><strong>Stages.</strong> First instance, appeal, cassation and enforcement may be priced separately.</li>
          <li><strong>Urgent applications.</strong> Temporary alimony or a travel order adds work at the start.</li>
          <li><strong>Documents.</strong> Legal translation and legalisation of foreign documents cost extra.</li>
          <li><strong>Who does the work.</strong> A senior advocate or a team; ask, because it affects the price.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>How lawyers structure fees</h3>
        <p>
          Lawyers may quote a fixed fee for the whole matter, a fee for each stage, or an hourly rate for
          complex work. Get the structure, and what is not included, in the written agreement. How lawyers charge,
          and how to budget, is set out in
          <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-fees-uae/">divorce lawyer fees in the UAE</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first consultation arranged through this site is free. Elsewhere, some lawyers charge for a first
          meeting; what to expect is in
          <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-consultation-fee-uae/">divorce lawyer consultation fees in the UAE</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ===== CHEAP LAWYER ===== -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="cheap-heading">
        <h2 id="cheap-heading">Looking for a Cheap Divorce Lawyer in Dubai? Honest Options</h2>
        <p>
          Wanting to keep costs down is reasonable. With Dubai’s family court fees capped at AED 2,000, the
          lawyer is where the money goes, and the way to spend less is to buy only the help you need, not to
          pick the lowest quote without knowing what it covers. No route promises a particular result.
        </p>
        <ul class="list">
          <li><strong>Check free and low-cost help first.</strong> What exists, and who qualifies, is set out in <a href="/en/divorce-legal-aid-uae/">divorce legal aid in the UAE</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Pay for one task, not the whole case.</strong> A single strategy consultation, a settlement review, or drafting only.</li>
          <li><strong>Represent yourself, or use a relative.</strong> You may appear in person, or authorise a relative or in-law up to the fourth degree (Article 52 of the Civil Procedure Code; Article 8(1) of Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022). When this works is covered in <a href="/en/do-you-need-a-lawyer-for-divorce-uae/">do you need a lawyer for divorce in the UAE</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Agree what you can.</strong> Dubai Courts’ Family Conciliation service has no fee, and every issue you settle is one less to argue.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>False economies to avoid</h3>
        <ul class="list">
          <li>Anyone not on a Dubai advocates’ roll offering to appear for you. Apart from you and the relatives the law allows, appearance is reserved to licensed lawyers (Article 6).</li>
          <li>One lawyer for both spouses: not allowed (Article 45(1)(f)).</li>
          <li>Signing a settlement unreviewed. An approved Family Guidance settlement is enforceable like a judgment (Article 8(2) of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024).</li>
          <li>A low quote that covers only the first hearing.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- ===== ABROAD ===== -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="abroad-heading">
        <h2 id="abroad-heading">If You Live Abroad or Your Spouse Has Left the UAE</h2>
        <p>
          A Dubai divorce does not always need you in the room. Under the Civil Procedure Code, steps from
          registration to judgment, appeal and execution are valid when done wholly or partly remotely
          (Article 330), including from outside the emirate (Article 332). If you file from outside the UAE
          with no domicile here, you must name an elected domicile and a legal representative (Article
          44(2)(a)); a lawyer acting under a power of attorney can fill that role.
        </p>
        <p>
          If your husband is a foreigner who lived or worked in the UAE and then left, Article 4(2) of Federal
          Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 lets UAE courts hear a divorce case filed by a wife resident in the State
          against a husband who abandoned her and moved abroad, was deported, or whose domicile abroad is
          unknown. Service by modern means, and failing that by posting on the court’s website, is allowed
          (Article 9(1)(a) of the Civil Procedure Code). These cases turn on proof of service, which is
          lawyer’s work.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ===== HOW WE FIT IN ===== -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="referral-heading">
        <h2 id="referral-heading">How DivorceU.AE Fits In</h2>
        <p>
          DivorceU.AE is an information and referral service, not a law firm. We do not give legal advice or
          represent anyone in court. When you fill in the form, your details go only to a UAE-licensed family
          lawyer matched to your case, and the first consultation is free.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you instruct that lawyer, the written fee agreement is between you and the lawyer, and the advice
          is theirs. You are free to say no and to check the lawyer’s registration before you sign. You can
          tell us your preferred language, or that you would prefer a female lawyer; we try to match that but
          cannot promise it.
        </p>
        <p class="page-note">
          This page is general information, updated November 2026. It is not legal advice; a licensed lawyer
          who has seen your documents can tell you how the law applies to you.
        </p>
      </section>

